{:edb/db-before-t 1492 :edb/db-after-t 1493 :edb/tx-data [[13194139534805 50 #inst "2026-10-08T07:33:44.382Z" 13194139534805 true] [17592186105386 1100 "Find a vector equation of the straight line passing through $P(3,-1,7)$ and parallel to $[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 5 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]$" 13194139534805 true] [17592186105386 1100 "Find a vector equation of the straight line passing through $P(3,-1,7)$ and parallel to $\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 5 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}$" 13194139534805 false] [17592186105386 1145 "The vector equation of the straight line passing through $P$ and parallel to $\\mathbf{v}$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r} = \\mathbf{p} + t \\mathbf{v}\n$$\n\nwhere $t \\in (-\\infty, \\infty)$ and $\\mathbf{p}$ is the position vector of $P$.\n\nIn our case, the position vector of the point $P$ is $\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 7\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nUsing the formula for the vector equation of a line, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nr &= \\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 7\\end{bmatrix} + t\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 5 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}, t ∈ (- ∞, ∞)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534805 false] [17592186105386 1145 "The vector equation of the straight line passing through $P$ and parallel to $\\mathbf{v}$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r} = \\mathbf{p} + t \\mathbf{v},\n$$\n\nwhere $t \\in (-\\infty, \\infty)$ and $\\mathbf{p}$ is the position vector of $P$.\n\nIn our case, the position vector of the point $P$ is $[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}]$\n\nUsing the formula for the vector equation of a line, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}]+t[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 5 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}],\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534805 true] [17592186105387 1100 "Find a vector equation of the line that passes through the points $P(1,-1,2)$ and $Q(3,2,7)$." 13194139534805 false] [17592186105387 1100 "Find a vector equation of the line that passes through the points $P(1,-1,2)$ and $Q(3,2,7).$" 13194139534805 true] [17592186105387 1145 "The vector equation of the straight line passing through $P$ and parallel to $\\mathbf{v}$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r} = \\mathbf{p} + t \\mathbf{v}\n$$\n\nwhere $t \\in (-\\infty, \\infty)$ and $\\mathbf{p}$ is the position vector of $P$.\n\nWe can find a direction vector by computing the displacement vector between the given points $P(1,-1,2)$ and $Q(3,2,7)$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nv &= PQ^(→) \\\\\n&= ⟨3, 2, 7⟩ - ⟨1,-1, 2⟩ \\\\\n&= ⟨2, 3, 5⟩\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the vector equation of the line is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nr &= \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix} + t\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}, t ∈ (- ∞, ∞)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534805 false] [17592186105387 1145 "The vector equation of the straight line passing through $P$ and parallel to $\\mathbf{v}$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r} = \\mathbf{p} + t \\mathbf{v},\n$$\n\nwhere $t \\in (-\\infty, \\infty)$ and $\\mathbf{p}$ is the position vector of $P.$ \n\nWe can find a direction vector by computing the displacement vector between the given points $P(1,-1,2)$ and $Q(3,2,7),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =\\overset{\\to }{PQ} \\\\  & =⟨3,2,7⟩-⟨1,-1,2⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2,3,5⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the vector equation of the line is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]+t[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}],\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534805 true] [17592186105388 1100 "Consider the straight line that passes through $P(1,2,0)$ and parallel to $\\mathbf{v}= \\langle -4,1,1 \\rangle$. Find all the possible points $A$ on that line such that $PA=6\\sqrt{2}$." 13194139534805 false] [17592186105388 1100 "Consider the straight line that passes through $P(1,2,0)$ and parallel to $\\mathbf{v}= \\langle -4,1,1 \\rangle.$ Find all the possible points $A$ on that line such that $PA=6\\sqrt{2}.$" 13194139534805 true] [17592186105388 1145 "First, we find the vector equation of the line, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nr &= p + tv \\\\\nr &= ⟨1, 2, 0⟩ + t⟨ - 4, 1, 1⟩\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $t \\in (-\\infty, \\infty)$.\n\nSince $A$ lies on the line, we know that there must be a real number $t$ such that\n\n$$\n\\overrightarrow{OA} = \\langle 1, 2, 0 \\rangle + t \\langle -4, 1, 1 \\rangle\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nPA^(→) &= OA^(→) - OP^(→) \\\\\n&= (⟨1, 2, 0⟩ + t⟨ - 4, 1, 1⟩) - ⟨1, 2, 0⟩ \\\\\n&= t⟨ - 4, 1, 1⟩ \\\\\n&= ⟨ - 4t, t, t⟩\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that\n\n$$\nPA=6\\sqrt{2}\n$$\n\nwe get:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nPA &= 6\\sqrt{2} \\\\\n\\mid PA^(→) \\mid &= 6\\sqrt{2} \\\\\n\\mid ⟨ - 4t, t, t⟩ \\mid &= 6\\sqrt{2} \\\\\n\\sqrt{(-4t)^{2} + t^{2} + t^{2}} &= 6\\sqrt{2} \\\\\n\\sqrt{18t^{2}} &= 6\\sqrt{2} \\\\\n± 3t\\sqrt{2} &= 6\\sqrt{2} \\\\\nt &= ± 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, putting the values $t=\\pm 2$ into the equation for the line, we obtain\n\n$$\nOA_{1}^(→) = ⟨1, 2, 0⟩ + 2⟨ - 4, 1, 1⟩ = ⟨ - 7, 4, 2⟩,; OA_{2}^(→) = ⟨1, 2, 0⟩ - 2⟨ - 4, 1, 1⟩ = ⟨9, 0,-2⟩\n$$\n\nTherefore, the points are $A_1(-7, 4, 2)$ and $A_2(9, 0, -2)$." 13194139534805 false] [17592186105388 1145 "First, we find the vector equation of the line, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =p+tv \\\\ r & =⟨1,2,0⟩+t⟨-4,1,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $t \\in (-\\infty, \\infty).$\n\nSince $A$ lies on the line, we know that there must be a real number $t$ such that\n\n$$\n\\overrightarrow{OA} = \\langle 1, 2, 0 \\rangle + t \\langle -4, 1, 1 \\rangle\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{PA} & =\\overset{\\to }{OA}-\\overset{\\to }{OP} \\\\  & =(⟨1,2,0⟩+t⟨-4,1,1⟩)-⟨1,2,0⟩ \\\\  & =t\\,⟨-4,\\,1,\\,1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-4t,\\,t,\\,t⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that $PA=6\\sqrt{2},$ we get:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}PA & =6\\sqrt{2} \\\\ |\\overset{\\to }{PA}| & =6\\sqrt{2} \\\\ |⟨-4t,\\,t,\\,t⟩| & =6\\sqrt{2} \\\\ \\sqrt{(-4t)^{2}+{t}^{2}+{t}^{2}} & =6\\sqrt{2} \\\\ \\sqrt{18{t}^{2}} & =6\\sqrt{2} \\\\ \\pm 3t\\sqrt{2} & =6\\sqrt{2} \\\\ t & =\\pm 2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, putting the values $t=\\pm 2$ into the equation for the line, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\overset{\\to }{O{A}_{1}}=⟨1,2,0⟩+2⟨-4,1,1⟩=⟨-7,4,2⟩, \\\\  & \\overset{\\to }{O{A}_{2}}=⟨1,2,0⟩-2⟨-4,1,1⟩=⟨9,0,-2⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the points are $A_1(-7, 4, 2)$ and $A_2(9, 0, -2).$" 13194139534805 true] [17592186105389 1100 "Using $\\mathbf{i},\\mathbf{j},\\mathbf{k}$ notation, find a parametrization of the line segment $\\overline{PQ}$, where $P(-4,1,2)$ and $Q(-2,5,-1)$." 13194139534805 false] [17592186105389 1100 "Using $\\mathbf{i},\\mathbf{j},\\mathbf{k}$ notation, find a parametrization of the line segment $\\overline{PQ},$ where $P(-4,1,2)$ and $Q(-2,5,-1).$" 13194139534805 true] [17592186105389 1145 "We need to find a direction vector that is parallel to the given line. We can use the vector $\\overrightarrow{PQ}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nv &= PQ^(→) \\\\\n&= OQ^(→) - OP^(→) \\\\\n&= (-2i + 5j - k) - (-4i + j + 2k) \\\\\n&= 2i + 4j - 3k\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the vector equation of the line is\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r} = \\mathbf{p} + t \\mathbf{v} \\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad \\mathbf{r} = (-4 \\, \\mathbf{i} + \\mathbf{j} + 2\\,\\mathbf{k}) + t (2 \\, \\mathbf{i} + 4 \\, \\mathbf{j} - 3 \\, \\mathbf{k})\n$$\n\nand the parametrization of the line segment $\\overline{PQ}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nr(t) &= (-4i + j + 2k) + t(2i + 4j - 3k) \\\\\n&= (2t - 4)i + (4t + 1)j + (2 - 3t)k, t ∈ [0, 1]\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534805 false] [17592186105389 1145 "We need to find a direction vector that is parallel to the given line. We can use the vector $\\overrightarrow{PQ}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =\\overset{\\to }{PQ} \\\\  & =\\overset{\\to }{OQ}-\\overset{\\to }{OP} \\\\  & =(-2\\,i+5\\,j-k)-(-4\\,i+j+2\\,k) \\\\  & =2\\,i+4\\,j-3\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the vector equation of the line is\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r} = \\mathbf{p} + t \\mathbf{v} \n\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad\n\\mathbf{r} = (-4 \\, \\mathbf{i} + \\mathbf{j} + 2\\,\\mathbf{k}) + t (2 \\, \\mathbf{i} + 4 \\, \\mathbf{j} - 3 \\, \\mathbf{k})\n$$\n\nand the parametrization of the line segment $\\overline{PQ}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =(-4\\,i+j+2\\,k)+t(2\\,i+4\\,j-3\\,k) \\\\  & =(2t-4)\\,i+(4t+1)\\,j+(2-3t)\\,k,\\,t\\in [0,1].\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534805 true] [17592186105821 1020 #edb/ref 17592186311097 13194139534805 true] [17592186105821 1020 #edb/ref 17592186311160 13194139534805 true] [17592186105821 1020 #edb/ref 17592186311167 13194139534805 true] [17592186105821 1020 #edb/ref 17592186311174 13194139534805 true] [17592186105822 1020 #edb/ref 17592186311104 13194139534805 true] [17592186105822 1020 #edb/ref 17592186311187 13194139534805 true] [17592186105822 1020 #edb/ref 17592186311194 13194139534805 true] [17592186105822 1020 #edb/ref 17592186311201 13194139534805 true] [17592186105823 1020 #edb/ref 17592186311111 13194139534805 true] [17592186105823 1020 #edb/ref 17592186311118 13194139534805 true] [17592186105823 1020 #edb/ref 17592186311208 13194139534805 true] [17592186105823 1020 #edb/ref 17592186311215 13194139534805 true] [17592186105824 1020 #edb/ref 17592186311125 13194139534805 true] [17592186105824 1020 #edb/ref 17592186311132 13194139534805 true] [17592186105824 1020 #edb/ref 17592186311139 13194139534805 true] [17592186105824 1020 #edb/ref 17592186311146 13194139534805 true] [17592186105824 1020 #edb/ref 17592186311153 13194139534805 true] [17592186106118 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139534805 true] [17592186106118 1100 "Consider the straight line that passes through $P(3, 1,-1)$ and is parallel to $v = ⟨2, 1, 3⟩$. Find all the possible points $A$ on that line such that $AP = \\sqrt{14}$." 13194139534805 false] [17592186106118 1100 "Consider the straight line that passes through $P(3,1,-1)$ and is parallel to $v=⟨2,1,3⟩.$ Find all the possible points $A$ on that line such that $AP=\\sqrt{14}.$" 13194139534805 true] [17592186106118 1145 "First, we find the vector equation of the line, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =p+tv \\\\ r & =⟨3,1,-1⟩+t⟨2,1,3⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$\n\nSince $A$ lies on the line, we know that there must be a real number $t$ such that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{OA} & =⟨3,1,-1⟩+t⟨2,1,3⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{PA} & =\\overset{\\to }{OA}-\\overset{\\to }{OP} \\\\  & =(⟨3,1,-1⟩+t⟨2,1,3⟩)-⟨3,1,-1⟩ \\\\  & =t\\,⟨2,1,3⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2t,\\,t,\\,3t⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that $PA=\\sqrt{14},$ we get:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}AP & =\\sqrt{14} \\\\ |\\overset{\\to }{PA}| & =\\sqrt{14} \\\\ |⟨2t,\\,t,\\,3t⟩| & =\\sqrt{14} \\\\ \\sqrt{(2t)^{2}+{t}^{2}+(3t)^{2}} & =\\sqrt{14} \\\\ \\sqrt{14{t}^{2}} & =\\sqrt{14} \\\\ \\pm t\\sqrt{14} & =\\sqrt{14} \\\\ t & =\\pm 1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, putting the values $t=\\pm 1$ into the equation for the line, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\overset{\\to }{O{A}_{1}}=⟨3,1,-1⟩+1⟨2,1,3⟩=⟨5,2,2⟩, \\\\  & \\overset{\\to }{O{A}_{2}}=⟨3,1,-1⟩-1⟨2,1,3⟩=⟨1,0,-4⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the points are ${A}_{1}(5,2,2)$ and ${A}_{2}(1,0,-4).$" 13194139534805 true] [17592186106212 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139534805 true] [17592186106212 1100 "Find a vector equation of the straight line passing through $Q(\\sqrt{3}, \\frac{11}{2},-7)$ and parallel to $v = ⟨1, 1,-2⟩$." 13194139534805 false] [17592186106212 1100 "Find a vector equation of the straight line passing through $Q(\\sqrt{3},\\,\\frac{11}{2},\\,-7)$ and parallel to $v=⟨1,\\,1,\\,-2⟩.$" 13194139534805 true] [17592186106212 1145 "The vector equation of the straight line passing through $Q$ and parallel to $v$ is given by\n\n$$\nr=q+tv,\n$$\n\nwhere $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )$ and $q$ is the position vector of $Q.$ \n\nIn our case, the position vector of the point $Q$ is $q=⟨\\sqrt{3},\\,\\frac{11}{2},\\,-7⟩.$ \n\nUsing the formula for the vector equation of a line, we have\n\n$$\nr=⟨\\sqrt{3},\\,\\frac{11}{2},\\,-7⟩+t⟨1,\\,1,\\,-2⟩,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$" 13194139534805 true] [17592186106215 1017 #edb/ref 17592186103799 13194139534805 false] [17592186106215 1017 #edb/ref 17592186311181 13194139534805 true] [17592186106215 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139534805 true] [17592186106215 1100 "Find a vector equation of the line that passes through the points $P(0,-2,-4)$ and $Q(5, 0, 1)$." 13194139534805 false] [17592186106215 1100 "Find a vector equation of the line that passes through the points $P(0,-2,-4)$ and $Q(5,0,1).$" 13194139534805 true] [17592186106215 1145 "The vector equation of the straight line passing through $P$ and parallel to $v$ is given by\n\n$$\nr=p+tv,\n$$\n\nwhere $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )$ and $p$ is the position vector of $P.$ \n\nWe can find a direction vector by computing the displacement vector between the given points $P(0,-2,-4)$ and $Q(5,0,1)$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =\\overset{\\to }{PQ} \\\\  & =⟨5,0,1⟩-⟨0,-2,-4⟩ \\\\  & =⟨5,2,5⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the vector equation of the line is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -2 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}]+t[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}],\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534805 true] [17592186311097 1017 #edb/ref 17592186311098 13194139534805 true] [17592186311097 1089 "q-126430" 13194139534805 true] [17592186311097 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139534805 true] [17592186311097 1100 "Find a vector equation of the straight line passing through $P(3,-2,5)$ and parallel to $v=⟨4,1,6⟩.$" 13194139534805 true] [17592186311097 1113 #uuid "b6f778f2-0d6a-54b4-923f-915d79d5f301" 13194139534805 true] [17592186311097 1145 "The vector equation of the straight line passing through $P$ and parallel to $v$ is given by\n\n$$\nr=p+tv,\n$$\n\nwhere $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )$ and $p